<h1>Password Hashing vs Encryption: What Developers Should Know</h1>
<p>Password storage is one of the most common places where beginners mix up hashing and encryption. The difference is not academic. If a system stores passwords in a reversible encrypted form, anyone who gets the decryption key may recover the original passwords. If a system stores passwords with a strong password hashing method, login checks can still work without keeping recoverable passwords.</p>
<p>OWASP explains this distinction clearly in its <a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html">Password Storage Cheat Sheet</a>. Passwords should normally be hashed with modern adaptive algorithms rather than encrypted. The goal is to verify a future login attempt, not to read the user's original password again.</p>
<p>Here is the normal flow. When a user creates a password, the application hashes it with a password hashing algorithm and stores the result. Later, when the user logs in, the application hashes the submitted password using the stored parameters and compares the result. If the values match, the password was correct. The original password does not need to be stored or recovered.</p>
<p>Encryption has a different purpose. It is useful when data must be read again, such as a saved address, private note, or API secret. That data is encrypted and later decrypted with a key. This is not how normal password verification should work. If your application can show users their existing password, that is a warning sign.</p>
<p>A plain fast hash such as MD5 or SHA-256 is also not enough for password storage. Fast algorithms help attackers test many guesses after a leak. Password hashing algorithms such as bcrypt, Argon2id, scrypt, and PBKDF2 are designed to slow down guessing through work factors, memory cost, or iteration counts. This slows legitimate login slightly, but it slows large-scale attacks much more.</p>
<p>Salts are part of the same design. A salt is a unique random value used with each password hash. It prevents identical passwords from producing identical stored hashes and makes precomputed lookup tables less useful. Most modern password hashing libraries manage salts automatically when used correctly, so developers should avoid inventing a custom format unless they have a strong reason.</p>
<p>November is a good time for security cleanup before holiday traffic and end-of-year deployment freezes. Review whether your login system uses a current password hashing method. Check old admin panels, internal tools, and customer portals. Legacy code often hides weak password handling in places no one has touched for years.</p>
<p>A good password review should include database storage, reset emails, logs, analytics events, and support tools. Passwords should never appear in logs, exports, or customer support screens. Hashing protects stored verifiers, but it does not fix unsafe handling before the hash is created. Treat the full password lifecycle as part of the same security decision.</p>
<p>The practical rule is this: hash passwords with a dedicated password hashing algorithm; encrypt data only when you must recover it; never store plaintext passwords; and do not use MD5 for new password systems. If your system still contains MD5 password hashes, keep reading this series because a later guide explains how to migrate them safely.</p>
